{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fad0c55d06db28008baa651960d232212e40d1be8b8baaf429fe2b5d71bf0d97"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4},\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=1$\n- $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=1.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fad0c55d06db28008baa651960d232212e40d1be8b8baaf429fe2b5d71bf0d97"} {"question" "q-96990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-96990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-x$\n- ${y}^{‴}-y=x-{y}^{″}$\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x=y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fad0c55d06db28008baa651960d232212e40d1be8b8baaf429fe2b5d71bf0d97"} {"question" "q-118783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fad0c55d06db28008baa651960d232212e40d1be8b8baaf429fe2b5d71bf0d97"} {"question" "q-118858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2}=8$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}=3{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fad0c55d06db28008baa651960d232212e40d1be8b8baaf429fe2b5d71bf0d97"} {"question" "q-118868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f908f60026ad7a026ed4fbeb17eccaed996796224e8fb83a645de71"} {"question" "q-118868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "b0e23afd6f9